<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Round City of Baghdad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">762</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Round City, Madinat al-Salam, City of Peace</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Round City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, officially named Madinat al-Salam or the City of Peace, was the circular walled capital founded in 762 by the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mansur on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>. Its concentric walls enclosed the caliphal palace and great mosque at the centre, with gates facing the main routes of the empire. It became the core of Baghdad, which grew into one of the largest and wealthiest cities of the medieval world under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/round-city-of-baghdad.jpg" alt="Round City of Baghdad" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Muir · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baghdad_150_to_300_AH.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
